  "notes": "First Q-simple, geometrically split example of [2,2,2,4] for the census (the gsplit cell is empty). Found by querying the LMFDB genus 2 data (devmirror.lmfdb.xyz, g2c_curves_new: is_simple_base = true, is_simple_geom = false, torsion_subgroup = [2,2,2,4]; geom_end_alg = Q x Q, end_alg = Q; smallest conductor among the curves of this group that the census verifier certifies). LMFDB conductor 176400. Checked locally with the census's own pipeline/magma/verify_lib.m (Magma V2.30-1): J(Q)_tors = [2,2,2,4], class gsplit, Q-simplicity prime 19 (chi = T^4 - 10*T^2 + 361), splitness certificate: richelot over Q ((2,2)-isogenous over Q to a Weil restriction of an elliptic curve E over Q(sqrt(-5)) (the surface is E x E^sigma)). Note: Magma's genus-2 Conductor on the reduced minimal model gives 184968806400 (2-part by Ogg's formula), not the LMFDB value 176400. Submitted by F. Najman; the curve is due to the database cited under Reference. Rigorous conductor: J is (2,2)-isogenous over Q to Res_(K/Q) E with K the quadratic field of discriminant -20, so N(J) = |d_K|^2 * Norm(f_E) = 400 * 441 = 176400 (computed with Magma; conductor is an isogeny invariant).",
